course: arts
lesson: 21
title: Filters, edits and fake photos
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A photo looks like proof, but it is really a choice: someone picked where to point the camera, what to cut off, and often what to change afterwards with an app.
explain: Think of a cake that looks perfect online: the burnt edge was cut out of the picture, the light was made warmer, and a filter made the icing shine, yet nothing in the photo is "fake" on its own.
explain: Phone filters find your face in real time, which means instantly while you film, and stick on dog ears or smooth your skin; some are just jokes, and some quietly change how a face looks.
explain: So a smart viewer asks three questions: what was left out of the frame, what was changed, and where did this picture first come from?
explain: People say it their own way: "fam" is your mates or family, "proper class" is brilliant, and when Gen Z kids say a picture is "cooked" or "sus" they mean it looks wrong or suspicious.
fact: Many photos of famous people and influencers are edited with apps to smooth skin, slim bodies or change faces; real people have pores, spots and all sorts of shapes.
fact: A filter is an effect that changes how a photo or video looks; beauty filters can change face shape and skin without anyone saying so.
fact: Fake photos of sharks swimming in flooded streets have spread after many hurricanes; they were edited or taken from somewhere else.
fact: A reverse image search looks for a picture on the internet to find where it first came from, which can show that a "new" photo is old or fake.
fact: Cropping means cutting off the edges of a photo; it is a normal tool, but what is left out can change the story.
fact: Editing a photo of someone to mock them and sharing it is a kind of bullying, even if it was "just a joke".
fact: Photos were faked long before computers: in the Soviet Union, people who fell out of favour with the leader Stalin were removed from official photos by hand.
fact: Some apps change your face or age with AI, and some keep or use the photos people give them, so it is wise to ask a grown-up first.
fact: Some researchers report that heavy filter use can make young people feel worse about their looks; how big the effect is, is still argued.
fact: Forced perspective is a camera trick: something close to the camera looks huge and something far away looks tiny.
fact: Red eye happens when a camera flash bounces off the back of the eye, which is full of tiny blood vessels; it does not mean anything is wrong, and turning off the flash or looking slightly away from the camera avoids it.
exercise: Mia's sister says a celebrity's perfect photo is "edited, babe". What is most likely true? | Apps smoothed the skin and changed the shape | The celebrity really has perfect skin with no pores at all | Expensive cameras always make people look much slimmer | Famous people never have spots
exercise: What does a face filter do? | It finds your face and adds effects instantly | It films a different person instead of you | It only works on photos taken years ago | It makes the camera lens bigger
exercise: Arjun sees a shark swimming down a flooded motorway. What should he do before sharing? | Check where the photo first came from | Share it fast so his class sees it before anyone else does | Trust it, because a photo is always proof of what happened | Add a filter so the picture looks even more real and scary
exercise: Kayla cut the burnt edge out of her cake photo. What is this called? | Cropping | Forced perspective | Red eye | A filter
exercise: A classmate edits a photo to make a boy look silly and posts it. What is this? | A kind of bullying, even if called a joke | Just a harmless bit of fun | Art, so nobody can complain | Fine, as long as the photo was real first
exercise: Zara's teacher says some old photos were faked. Which is true? | Photos were changed by hand long before computers | Nobody in history could change a photo before phones came along | Only modern governments have ever faked photos, never in the past | Only artists could fake them
exercise: Ethan's app turns his selfie into an old man. What is wise before using such apps? | Ask a grown-up, because some apps keep your photos | Nothing, free apps never cost you anything | Upload as many family photos as possible | Give the app your full name and address
exercise: Holly's nan says "the one without" is the best selfie. What idea is she sharing? | You do not need a filter to look good | Filters are against the law for teenagers | Photos without filters cannot be posted | Nans cannot see filters on phones
exercise: Callum sees a dog as big as a horse. What trick might it be? | Forced perspective, with the dog near the camera | A special rare breed of dog that grows as big as a horse | Red eye from the camera flash making the dog look bigger | Cropping made the dog grow
exercise: Why do Sophie's eyes look red in the flash photo? | The flash bounces off the back of her eyes | Her eyes are ill and need a doctor | The camera adds red to look scary | Her eyes were tired from looking at screens
exercise: What is a reverse image search? | Searching the web with a picture to find its source | Turning a photo upside down and back to check if it is real | Searching for a photo by typing its main colour into a box | Deleting a photo everywhere
exercise: Which is the best clue that a shocking photo could be old or fake? | The same picture turns up years earlier elsewhere | It has lots of likes and shares from people all over the world | A friend you trust shared it in the class chat this morning | It looks very sharp and clear
exercise: Do researchers agree on how much filters affect how kids feel? | No, some report harm but how much is still argued | Yes, they all say filters cause no harm at all | Yes, they all say every filter is dangerous | No, because nobody has ever studied it
exercise: Leo's nan asks why he has a nose like a dog. What happened? | He used a funny dog filter | His phone camera is broken and changes noses at random | Nans see photos differently from kids because of their glasses | The photo was taken in forced perspective, close to the lens
exercise: How can you avoid red eye in photos? | Turn off the flash or look slightly away | Close one eye for every photo so the light cannot get in | Stand right next to the camera so the flash goes over you | Take the photo in total darkness with no light at all
exercise: Why was it unkind of the class to laugh at the edited photo? | It hurt the boy, and a hurtful joke is not just a joke | Because laughing is not allowed at school | Because the edit was not funny enough | Because only teachers may edit photos
test: Your mate goes, "A photo is proof, innit, cameras can't lie." What is the best answer? | Photos can be cropped, edited or taken from elsewhere | He is right, so every single photo you see online is true | Only black-and-white photos can be faked, colour ones cannot | Only video can be faked
test: What would happen if you cropped a picture of a messy room so only the tidy desk shows? | It would look like the whole room is tidy | The mess would show up anyway | The photo would be against the law | The desk would look messy too
test: Kid in Atlanta posts a giant cat next to a tiny house and goes "no cap, it's real" ("no cap" means honestly). What is the likely trick? | The cat was near the camera, the house far away | The cat really is as big as a house, some cats grow huge | The house was shrunk in the photo by the red-eye effect | The flash made the cat grow
test: In a cartoon, a selfie app makes a girl perfect and she says "now that's the real me". What is wrong? | The app changed her face; the real her is the unfiltered one | Nothing, apps show your true face better | Only her eyes are changed by apps | Apps cannot change faces, only colours
test: A Glaswegian lad says the shark-in-the-street picture is "pure mince, wee man" ("mince" means rubbish). What should the group do? | Check where it first appeared | Share it to more chats so everyone can decide for themselves | Believe it, because sharks can swim along flooded roads | Ignore every photo for ever after, since none can be trusted
test: Your cousin says photo faking is "a new thing from AI". Which is true? | People faked photos by hand long before computers | She is right, fake photos only began in the last few years | Faking only started when phones got cameras and editing apps | Faking is against the law
test: Someone edits your friend's face onto a pig and says "lighten up, it's bants" ("bants" means jokey teasing). What is the wise move? | Do not share it, support your friend and tell an adult | Share it, because it is only banter | Make an even worse one of the person who did it | Laugh along so nobody notices you
test: Which is true about the red eyes in a flash photo? | Light bounced off the back of the eye | The person was very angry at the moment the photo was taken | The camera has a hidden red filter switched on by mistake | It means the person needs glasses and should see an eye doctor
test: A free face app asks to save all your photos for ever. What is the smartest choice? | Ask a grown-up first and check the app | Say yes, because anything free must be safe for children to use | Give it extra photos of friends too, so the app works better | Turn off the phone for good
test: Holly's friend says, "If you post with no filter you're mid" ("mid" means average or boring). What is fair to say? | You do not need a filter to post a photo | She is right, everyone must filter every photo they ever post | Unfiltered photos are not allowed on most websites these days | Only nans and grandads post photos with no filter on them
test: Why might a photo of a perfect cake still be a bit misleading? | The burnt bits may have been cropped out | Cakes always look worse in real life than they do in any photo | Cameras cannot photograph food properly, so they add colours | Filters turn a badly baked cake into a really good cake
exam: Identify the principal reason for which photographs disseminated online ought not to be regarded as conclusive proof. | They may be cropped, edited or removed from context | Photographs are invariably produced by government agencies for propaganda | Photographs cannot be reproduced faithfully on electronic devices of any kind | Alteration of photographs is unlawful
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon designated "red eye"? | The reflection of a flash from the vascular rear surface of the eye | An indication of disease requiring medical attention | An effect deliberately introduced by all cameras | A consequence of excessive screen use by the subject
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the history of photographic falsification. | Photographs were altered by hand before computers | Photographic alteration became possible only in the present century with software | No photograph was ever altered in any way prior to the invention of mobile telephones | Historical photographs are exempt from any form of alteration by their very nature
exam: Ascertain the procedure most appropriate where a startling image is received prior to its onward transmission. | Verification of its original source prior to any sharing | Immediate forwarding to all available contacts | Application of a filter to enhance its realism | Acceptance of its authenticity on the basis of popularity
